arXiv:2602.20457v1 [cs.LG] 24 Feb 2026</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models
</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Zimeng Li
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mudit Gaur
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Vaneet Aggarwal
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Purdue University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We study online alignment of large language models under misspecified preference feedback, where the observed preference oracle deviates from an ideal but unknown ground-truth oracle. The online LLM alignment problem is a bi-level reinforcement problem due to the coupling between data collection and policy updates. Recently, the problem has been reduced to tractable single-level objective in the SAIL (Self-Improving Efficient Online Alignment) framework. In this paper, we introduce a pointwise oracle uncertainty set in this problem and formulate an oracle-robust online alignment objective as a worst-case optimization problem.
For log-linear policies, we show that this robust objective admits an exact closed-form decomposition into the original loss function plus an explicit sensitivity penalty.
We develop projected stochastic composite updates for the resulting weakly convex objective and prove <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\varepsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\varepsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> oracle complexity for reaching approximate stationarity.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id1" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">K</em><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eywords</span> LLM alignment  <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
preference learning  <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
oracle robustness  <math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
weakly convex optimization</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as interactive systems, where failures in instruction following or safety can have immediate impact. A common alignment pipeline is reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF), which updates a policy <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> using pairwise preference feedback on sampled responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al. (2017)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stiennon et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2022)</a></cite>. In practice, the feedback is produced by a preference oracle <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>, which may be a pool of human annotators or a learned reward/preference model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al. (2022)</a></cite>. Such oracles can deviate from an idealized true oracle <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> in structured ways, for example due to population heterogeneity across users or temporal drift in labeling standards, often mediated by style/value confounders (e.g., preferring verbosity over correctness), thereby inducing preference shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Poddar et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In offline alignment with a fixed preference dataset, this manifests as a train/test preference mismatch between the dataset and the deployed user population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a></cite>. In online or on-policy alignment, the policy <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> controls the distribution of queried comparisons, so systematic oracle deviations can be amplified by the feedback loop and lead to over-optimization of oracle quirks rather than the intended preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skalse et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karwowski et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eisenstein et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Therefore, robustness should be modeled explicitly through an uncertainty set and a worst-case objective, rather than via simplistic i.i.d. label-noise assumptions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing work on LLM alignment robustness largely falls into two categories. On the one hand, online RLHF methods explicitly account for the coupling between data collection and policy updates, using bilevel formulations that reduce to tractable single-level objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, these approaches typically assume the oracle matches the modeling assumptions and do not provide distributionally robust guarantees under oracle misspecification. On the other hand, recent offline methods robustify direct preference optimization (DPO) on a fixed dataset by solving a minimax problem over a distributional uncertainty set, improving performance under preference shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. These robust DPO formulations are tailored to static datasets and do not address the on-policy setting where the comparison distribution changes with <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, and where oracle perturbations can interact with the evolving sampling distribution. This gap motivates a robustness model that is compatible with online preference collection and still yields a tractable objective and optimization theory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The authors of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. (2024)</a></cite> proposed an approach, Self-Improving Efficient Online Alignment (SAIL), that reduces the bi-level reinforcement learning problem of LLM alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chakraborty et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gaur et al. (2025)</a></cite>
to an efficient single-level first-order method using the reward-policy equivalence approach. In this paper, we aim to consider this problem in the presence of oracle misspecification. We introduce a pointwise uncertainty set <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> that bounds the deviation of the preference probability <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(1\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(1\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}(1\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}(1\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> for every comparison <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> that may be generated under the policy-induced sampling distribution <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then define an oracle-robust objective <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as the worst-case negative log-likelihood over <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in\mathcal{U}^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in\mathcal{U}^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>, which guards against worst-case exploitation of structured oracle deviations in the on-policy feedback loop. Although robust bilevel formulations are generally difficult, under the log-linear preference model we show that <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> admits an exact closed-form decomposition into the nominal loss <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> plus an explicit robustness penalty <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\!\big[\lvert R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})\rvert\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\!\big[\lvert R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})\rvert\big]</annotation></semantics></math> is the expected absolute pairwise score and <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\rho\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\rho\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since the constrained objective can be non-smooth, we measure first-order stationarity via the Moreau-envelope <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>. By standard envelope/proximal properties, an <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of <math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that the associated proximal point is <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-nearly stationary for the original constrained problem (and the iterate lies within <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(\epsilon\lambda_{env})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(\epsilon\lambda_{env})</annotation></semantics></math> of the proximal point). Using this stationarity surrogate, we obtain an <math id="S1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\varepsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\varepsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> oracle complexity bound for reaching an <math id="S1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of the envelope. Finally, because <math id="S1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the nominal SAIL objective, our analysis also yields a convergence-to-stationarity guarantee for optimizing <math id="S1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as a special case.
We summarize our main contributions as follows.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate oracle-robust online alignment by combining SAIL with a pointwise oracle uncertainty set <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>, leading to the robust objective <math id="S1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> that optimizes against worst-case preference perturbations under policy-induced sampling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We give an exact closed-form characterization of <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and interpret <math id="S1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as an explicit sensitivity penalty given by an expected absolute pairwise score term.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show the resulting constrained robust objective is weakly convex and analyze projected stochastic composite updates using the Moreau-envelope as a smooth stationarity surrogate. We prove an <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\varepsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathcal{O}}(\varepsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> oracle complexity bound for reaching an <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of the envelope.
As a corollary, setting <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math> yields a convergence-to-stationarity guarantee for the original SAIL objective.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bilevel reinforcement learning and RLHF.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bilevel optimization provides a principled abstraction for hierarchical learning problems such as hyperparameter optimization and meta-learning, where an upper-level objective depends on the solution of a lower-level training problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Domke, 2012</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maclaurin et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Franceschi et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.
This perspective is increasingly relevant to alignment: RLHF couples (i) learning preferences/rewards and (ii) policy optimization (often implemented with PPO/TRPO-style updates), and the alignment objective is evaluated on data whose distribution is induced by the policy produced by the lower-level optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2015</a>)</cite>.
PARL (Policy Alignment in
Reinforcement Learning) formalizes policy alignment in RL as a stochastic bilevel program that explicitly accounts for decision-dependent data collection at the upper level, and develops an algorithm with finite-sample guarantees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chakraborty et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Focusing on online LLM alignment, SAIL similarly argues that the alignment process is underpinned by bilevel optimization, and derives an efficient single-level first-order surrogate via reward–policy equivalence, enabling iterative on-policy data generation and self-improving alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Complementing these algorithmic frameworks, recent theory studies the statistical and computational limits of bilevel RL in nonconvex settings; for example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gaur et al. (2025)</a></cite> establish sample complexity bounds for bilevel reinforcement learning with parameterized settings.
Related developments on general nonconvex bilevel optimization further analyze and mitigate the cost of hypergradient computation through penalty-based approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen and Chen, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robust alignment under distribution shift.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central challenge in alignment is robustness: preference data are typically collected from a narrow, static source distribution, while deployment-time preferences can vary across populations and drift over time, causing brittleness for offline objectives such as DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Son et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
A recent line of work imports distributionally robust optimization (DRO)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rahimian and Mehrotra (2019)</a></cite> to explicitly hedge against such preference shifts.
In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2025)</a></cite> propose distributionally robust DPO with Wasserstein and KL uncertainty sets (WDPO/KLDPO), provide sample-complexity characterizations, and develop scalable gradient-based algorithms suitable for large-scale LLM fine-tuning.
Concurrently, robust variants of direct preference learning consider alternative uncertainty sets and regularizers; e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite> distributionally robustify DPO and empirically study robustness to preference/data perturbations, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al. (2024)</a></cite> optimize for worst-case group mixtures to handle heterogeneous preferences.
These methods connect robust alignment to foundational DRO results and tractable reformulations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohajerin Esfahani and Kuhn, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuhn et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duchi and Namkoong, 2021</a>)</cite>, as well as classical robust RL/MDP formulations that optimize against worst-case transition models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Iyengar, 2005</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Setup</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the prompt (context) space and <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> the response space. For each parameter vector <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, the language model induces a conditional distribution (policy) <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)\in\Delta(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)\in\Delta(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(Y)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the probability simplex over <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. We view each <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in Y</annotation></semantics></math> as a finite token sequence and assume <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated autoregressively, i.e.,
<math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)=\prod_{t=1}^{|y|}\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)=\prod_{t=1}^{|y|}\pi_{\theta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{&lt;t}),</annotation></semantics></math>
with the usual convention that generation terminates at an end-of-sequence event.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We model preferences over response pairs by a (possibly unknown) preference oracle
<math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}:=\Bigl\{P\Bigm|\ P(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)=1-P(y_{2}\succ y_{1}\mid x)\Bigr\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}:=\Bigl\{P\Bigm|\ P(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)=1-P(y_{2}\succ y_{1}\mid x)\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
For <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in Z:=X\times Y\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in Z:=X\times Y\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>, we write <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(1\mid z):=P(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(1\mid z):=P(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> and interpret the (stochastic) preference label <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{I}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{I}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y\mid z\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}\!\bigl(P(1\mid z)\bigr),\mathbb{I}=1\Leftrightarrow y_{1}\succ y_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mpadded style="width:4.221em;" width="4.221em"><mi>Bernoulli</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>𝕀</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">⇔</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\mid z\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}\!\bigl(P(1\mid z)\bigr),\mathbb{I}=1\Leftrightarrow y_{1}\succ y_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The sampling procedure is policy-dependent: draw a prompt <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math> from a fixed distribution <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, then sample two responses independently from <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x\sim\mu,\;y_{1},y_{2}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">iid</mo></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\mu,\;y_{1},y_{2}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x).</annotation></semantics></math>
This induces the data-generating distribution on <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}):=\mu(x)\,\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}\mid x)\,\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}\mid x)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}):=\mu(x)\,\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}\mid x)\,\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}\mid x).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}\in\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}\in\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the (unknown) true oracle. Fix <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>, define the pointwise uncertainty set</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}_{z}(P^{\star},\rho):=\Bigl\{P\in\mathcal{O}\ \Bigm|\ \bigl|P(1\mid z)-P^{\star}(1\mid z)\bigr|\leq\rho\Bigr\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" mathsize="1.600em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}_{z}(P^{\star},\rho):=\Bigl\{P\in\mathcal{O}\ \Bigm|\ \bigl|P(1\mid z)-P^{\star}(1\mid z)\bigr|\leq\rho\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Although <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}_{z}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}_{z}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> is specified as a pointwise (uniform) neighborhood in the scalar preference probability, it admits a Wasserstein interpretation at fixed <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peyré et al. (2019)</a></cite>: viewing <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P(\cdot\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\cdot\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P^{\star}(\cdot\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}(\cdot\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> as Bernoulli distributions on <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> with ground cost <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c(y,y^{\prime})=|y-y^{\prime}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c(y,y^{\prime})=|y-y^{\prime}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Wasserstein distance satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Ber}(P(1\mid z)),\mathrm{Ber}(P^{\star}(1\mid z))\bigr)=|P(1\mid z)-P^{\star}(1\mid z)|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Ber</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}\!\bigl(\mathrm{Ber}(P(1\mid z)),\mathrm{Ber}(P^{\star}(1\mid z))\bigr)=|P(1\mid z)-P^{\star}(1\mid z)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Hence, under binary support, the Wasserstein ball constraint <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}(\cdot,\cdot)\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}(\cdot,\cdot)\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the interval constraint in (<a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The corresponding global uncertainty set requires the pointwise constraint to hold uniformly over all comparison triples:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\bigcap_{z\in Z}U^{W}_{z}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋂</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mi>z</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\bigcap_{z\in Z}U^{W}_{z}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\Bigl\{P\in\mathcal{O}\ \Bigm|\ \sup_{z\in Z}\bigl|P(\cdot\mid z)-P^{\star}(\cdot\mid z)\bigr|\leq\rho\Bigr\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.500em" maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" rspace="0.389em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\Bigl\{P\in\mathcal{O}\ \Bigm|\ \sup_{z\in Z}\bigl|P(\cdot\mid z)-P^{\star}(\cdot\mid z)\bigr|\leq\rho\Bigr\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> models an adversarial but uniformly bounded misspecification of the (conditional) preference probability across all prompts and response pairs that may be encountered under the policy-induced sampling in (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Fix a prompt (context) <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math>. The policy <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math> independently generates a pair of responses
<math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, which are then compared by a (possibly noisy) preference oracle.
Although the latent reward function <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{\star}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{\star}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> underlying preferences is not directly observed, we assume the
oracle satisfies the Bradley–Terry (BT) model<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bradley and Terry (1952)</a></cite>: for all <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in X</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\in Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\in Y</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)=\sigma\!\bigl(r^{\star}(x,y_{1})-r^{\star}(x,y_{2})\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)=\sigma\!\bigl(r^{\star}(x,y_{1})-r^{\star}(x,y_{2})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(u):=(1+e^{-u})^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(u):=(1+e^{-u})^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the logistic sigmoid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We impose a mild margin condition to ensure that all admissible oracles remain valid. We state this non-degeneracy requirement in the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="Thmassumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Nondegenerate true oracle and admissible radius)<span id="Thmassumption1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1/2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1/2]</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in X\times Y\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in X\times Y\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\ \leq\ P^{\star}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\ \leq\ 1-\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\ \leq\ P^{\star}(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\ \leq\ 1-\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We restrict the oracle uncertainty radius to <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(0,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(0,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, so that every preference oracle <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is nondegenerate, i.e. <math id="Thmassumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall(x,y_{1},y_{2}),\ \forall P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∀</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall(x,y_{1},y_{2}),\ \forall P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq\delta-\rho\ \leq\ P(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\ \leq\ 1-(\delta-\rho)\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq\delta-\rho\ \leq\ P(y_{1}\succ y_{2}\mid x)\ \leq\ 1-(\delta-\rho)\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Nondegenerate true oracle and admissible radius). ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> rules out <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nearly deterministic</em> preferences: for every comparison <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the oracle assigns nontrivial probability to either outcome.
When the oracle obeys the Bradley–Terry form (<a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), this condition is equivalent to a uniform bound on the reward gap.
Indeed, writing <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta r^{\star}:=r^{\star}(x,y_{1})-r^{\star}(x,y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta r^{\star}:=r^{\star}(x,y_{1})-r^{\star}(x,y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the monotonicity of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> gives
<math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log\!\frac{\delta}{1-\delta}\ \leq\ \Delta r^{\star}\ \leq\ \log\!\frac{1-\delta}{\delta}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log\!\frac{\delta}{1-\delta}\ \leq\ \Delta r^{\star}\ \leq\ \log\!\frac{1-\delta}{\delta},</annotation></semantics></math>
and therefore</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl|r^{\star}(x,y_{1})-r^{\star}(x,y_{2})\bigr|\leq\log\!\frac{1-\delta}{\delta}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mi>δ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|r^{\star}(x,y_{1})-r^{\star}(x,y_{2})\bigr|\leq\log\!\frac{1-\delta}{\delta}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are drawn i.i.d. from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, (<a href="#S3.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ Remark 3.1. ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) can be read as a structural condition on the comparisons induced by the policy: the sampling procedure does not generate response pairs whose latent rewards differ so drastically that the preference label becomes essentially deterministic.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Background (SAIL).</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">SAIL is a preference-based RLHF framework for online alignment that explicitly models the coupling between (i) learning from pairwise preference feedback and (ii) updating the policy that generates the responses being compared. In the online regime, the preference data distribution is policy-dependent, and SAIL represents this dependence via a bilevel program: a reward model <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is fit from Bradley–Terry comparisons on responses sampled from the KL-regularized optimal policy induced by <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(upper)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(upper)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(upper)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{r}-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\,y_{i}\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x),(y_{w}\succ y_{\ell})\sim P^{\star}}\Bigl[\log\sigma\bigl(r(x,y_{w})-r(x,y_{\ell})\bigr)\Bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>≻</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{r}-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\,y_{i}\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x),(y_{w}\succ y_{\ell})\sim P^{\star}}\Bigl[\log\sigma\bigl(r(x,y_{w})-r(x,y_{\ell})\bigr)\Bigr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{(lower)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>(lower)</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{(lower)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\ \pi_{r}^{\star}\in\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\,y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\Bigl[r(x,y)-\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\bigl(\pi(\cdot\mid x)\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(\cdot\mid x)\bigr)\Bigr]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>arg</mi><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>π</mi></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\ \pi_{r}^{\star}\in\arg\max_{\pi}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\,y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\Bigl[r(x,y)-\beta D_{\mathrm{KL}}\!\bigl(\pi(\cdot\mid x)\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(\cdot\mid x)\bigr)\Bigr],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Direct differentiation through the inner solution mapping <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\mapsto\pi_{r}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\mapsto\pi_{r}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> requires hypergradient computations. SAIL circumvents this by exploiting the reward–policy equivalence for KL-regularized RL: any optimizer <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{r}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{r}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y)=\beta\log\frac{\pi_{r}^{\star}(y\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y\mid x)}+\beta\log Z_{r}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y)=\beta\log\frac{\pi_{r}^{\star}(y\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y\mid x)}+\beta\log Z_{r}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">for a normalization <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{r}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{r}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> independent of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting (<a href="#S3.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ Background (SAIL). ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) into the upper-level BT likelihood reduces the bilevel program to a tractable single-level policy objective; parametrizing <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta):=-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\;y_{1},y_{2}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x),\;(y_{w},y_{\ell})\sim P^{\star}}\!\left[\log\sigma\!\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{w}\mid x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}\right)\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mo mathsize="0.710em">iid</mo></mover><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>(</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta):=-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\;y_{1},y_{2}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x),\;(y_{w},y_{\ell})\sim P^{\star}}\!\left[\log\sigma\!\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{w}\mid x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">The single-level SAIL objective <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ Background (SAIL). ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) evaluates a policy <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> under preference feedback generated by the true oracle <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. In practice, however, we do not have access to <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the observed preference labels may be produced by a perturbed oracle whose conditional preference probabilities deviate from those of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>. We model this misspecification by allowing the data-generating oracle <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to range over the global uncertainty set <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. (<a href="#S3.E3" title="Equation 3 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>), which enforces a uniform pointwise deviation bound across all prompts and response pairs that may be encountered under policy-induced sampling. Concretely, given <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, we draw <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mtext>i.i.d.</mtext></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the oracle <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> induces an ordered pair <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{w},y_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{w},y_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the preferred and less preferred response. We then define the oracle-robust alignment objective as the worst-case SAIL value over <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta):=\sup_{P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)}-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\;y_{1},y_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x),\;(y_{w},y_{\ell})\sim P}\Biggl[\log\sigma\!\Biggl(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{w}\mid x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}\Biggr)\Biggr]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mtext mathsize="1.400em">i.i.d.</mtext></mover><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta):=\sup_{P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)}-\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu,\;y_{1},y_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x),\;(y_{w},y_{\ell})\sim P}\Biggl[\log\sigma\!\Biggl(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{w}\mid x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{\ell}\mid x)}\Biggr)\Biggr].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Proposed Approach</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first relate our oracle-robust objective <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> to the nominal SAIL objective
<math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math> by showing that <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> admits an <em id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact</em> decomposition into the SAIL loss
and an explicit regularization term. We then study the regularity of the two components separately:
we impose a smoothness condition on <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math> and analyze the policy-dependent penalty <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under mild assumptions, these results together imply that <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is weakly convex.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Decomposition of <math id="S4.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our robust objective <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined via a worst-case expectation over the global oracle uncertainty set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>. The following assumption specifies the log-linear SAIL comparison model and enables an exact reduction of the inner worst-case problem to an explicit regularizer.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="Thmassumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Log-linear policy class)<span id="Thmassumption2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:X\times Y\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:X\times Y\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be a known <math id="Thmassumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional feature map
We consider the class of log-linear (softmax) policies</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi:=\left\{\pi_{\theta}:\ \pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)=\frac{\exp\bigl(\theta^{\top}\psi(x,y)\bigr)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in Y}\exp\bigl(\theta^{\top}\psi(x,y^{\prime})\bigr)}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi:=\left\{\pi_{\theta}:\ \pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)=\frac{\exp\bigl(\theta^{\top}\psi(x,y)\bigr)}{\sum_{y^{\prime}\in Y}\exp\bigl(\theta^{\top}\psi(x,y^{\prime})\bigr)}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For notational convenience, let <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> denote the fixed parameter of the reference (SFT) policy, i.e</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}\;=\;\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}}\;=\;\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Decomposition of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Recall <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the non-robust SAIL objective and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the robust objective defined by the worst-case oracle in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.Define the pairwise score</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2}):=(\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{ref}})^{\top}\!\bigl(\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2})\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2}):=(\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{ref}})^{\top}\!\bigl(\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and the robust penalty</span></p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu}\mathbb{E}_{y_{1},y_{2}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)}\left[\bigl|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})\bigr|\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mu}\mathbb{E}_{y_{1},y_{2}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)}\left[\bigl|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})\bigr|\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Nondegenerate true oracle and admissible radius). ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,<a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Log-linear policy class). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the robust objective admits the exact decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta),\qquad\lambda:=\rho\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta),\qquad\lambda:=\rho\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃)). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})\in Z</annotation></semantics></math>. Define the pairwise log-ratio score</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}):=\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{1}\mid x)}-\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{2}\mid x)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>SFT</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}):=\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{1}\mid x)}-\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}\mid x)}{\pi_{\mathrm{SFT}}(y_{2}\mid x)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and the two per-sample losses</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z):=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z):=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z):=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,h_{\theta}(x,y_{2},y_{1})\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.437em;" width="0.437em"><mi>σ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z):=-\log\sigma\!\bigl(\beta\,h_{\theta}(x,y_{2},y_{1})\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\star}(z):=P^{\star}(1\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\star}(z):=P^{\star}(1\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z):=P(1\mid z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z):=P(1\mid z)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the SAIL objective admits the representation</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\Big[p^{\star}(z)\,\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+\bigl(1-p^{\star}(z)\bigr)\,\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\Big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\Big[p^{\star}(z)\,\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+\bigl(1-p^{\star}(z)\bigr)\,\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\Big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">while the robust objective is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=\sup_{P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)}\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\Big[p(z)\,\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+\bigl(1-p(z)\bigr)\,\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\Big]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=\sup_{P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)}\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\Big[p(z)\,\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+\bigl(1-p(z)\bigr)\,\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\Big].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.5.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> enforces <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|p(z)-p^{\star}(z)|\leq\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p(z)-p^{\star}(z)|\leq\rho</annotation></semantics></math> pointwise, the inner supremum is separable across <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and (by linearity in <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(z)</annotation></semantics></math>) is attained at an endpoint, yielding</span></p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E14X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim P(\cdot\mid z)}\big[y\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+(1-y)\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\big]=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim P^{\star}(\cdot\mid z)}\big[y\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+(1-y)\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\big]+\rho\,\bigl|\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)-\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\bigr|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>P</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.170em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)}\mathbb{E}_{y\sim P(\cdot\mid z)}\big[y\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+(1-y)\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\big]=\mathbb{E}_{y\sim P^{\star}(\cdot\mid z)}\big[y\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)+(1-y)\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\big]+\rho\,\bigl|\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)-\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.6.1" class="ltx_text">Taking <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\rho\,\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\bigl|\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)-\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\bigr|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\rho\,\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\bigl|\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)-\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)\bigr|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.7" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.7.1" class="ltx_text">Finally,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)-\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)=\log\frac{\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}))}{\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}))}=-\beta\,h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell^{1}_{\theta}(z)-\ell^{0}_{\theta}(z)=\log\frac{\sigma(-\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}))}{\sigma(\beta h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}))}=-\beta\,h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.8.1" class="ltx_text">so the extra term equals <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\beta\,\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\left[|h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})|\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\beta\,\mathbb{E}_{z\sim d_{\theta}}\left[|h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})|\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under the log-linear policy assumption (Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Log-linear policy class). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})=R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\theta}(x,y_{1},y_{2})=R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> , hence
<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\rho\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\rho\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> </span>(Interpretation of the decomposition)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1 (Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃)). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> separates the robust objective into a nominal fitting term <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and an explicit robustness penalty <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> that depends on the policy-induced sampling distribution. The penalty <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> measures the expected magnitude of the pairwise score <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> over i.i.d. response pairs <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(y_{1},y_{2})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{1},y_{2})\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus quantifies sensitivity of the likelihood to adversarial perturbations of the pointwise preference probability. The uncertainty radius enters only through the linear prefactor <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\rho\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\rho\beta</annotation></semantics></math>: increasing <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> monotonically strengthens the penalty, while <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers the nominal objective <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Regularity of of <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math> . </h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior analyses of preference-based objectives often invoke stronger global conditions (e.g., PL-type geometries <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gaur et al. (2025)</a></cite>). Here we treat <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> under the following smoothness assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 3</span></span><span id="Thmassumption3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Smooth SAIL objective)<span id="Thmassumption3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The SAIL objective <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="Thmassumption3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz gradient:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)-\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta^{\prime})\|\leq L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|\;\forall\,\theta,\theta^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.447em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)-\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta^{\prime})\|\leq L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}\|\theta-\theta^{\prime}\|\;\forall\,\theta,\theta^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Smooth SAIL objective). ‣ 4.2 Regularity of of 𝐿^SAIL . ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> is a standard smoothness condition in first-order optimization, and it is routinely imposed in analyses of gradient-based methods for both convex and nonconvex objectives, including stochastic settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nesterov, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghadimi and Lan, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bottou et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. We adopt it here as a mild regularity requirement on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Weak convexity of <math id="S4.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It remains to understand the regularity of the robustness penalty <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>. Although <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})|</annotation></semantics></math> is a convex function of <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> for fixed <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, the expectation in <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is taken under the policy-dependent distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim d_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y_{1},y_{2})\sim d_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> (Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)). Since <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> itself depends on <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>, convexity of the pointwise quantity
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})|</annotation></semantics></math> does <em id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> carry over to <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This coupling can destroy convexity, as evidenced by the following example.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the simplest setting with a single prompt <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{x\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{x\}</annotation></semantics></math>, two responses <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=\{a,b\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=\{a,b\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and define features by <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x,a)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x,a)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(x,b)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(x,b)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so that for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})=\theta\bigl(\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2})\bigr)\in\{0,\pm\theta\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})=\theta\bigl(\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2})\bigr)\in\{0,\pm\theta\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let the policy be <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(a\mid x)=\sigma(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(a\mid x)=\sigma(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}(b\mid x)=\sigma(-\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}(b\mid x)=\sigma(-\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, and sample <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>∼</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">iid</mo></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\overset{\mathrm{iid}}{\sim}\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})|=|\theta|\mathbf{1}\{y_{1}\neq y_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|R(\theta;x,y_{1},y_{2})|=|\theta|\mathbf{1}\{y_{1}\neq y_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence</span></p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E15X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=|\theta|\Pr(y_{1}\neq y_{2})=2|\theta|\,\pi_{\theta}(a\mid x)\pi_{\theta}(b\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo fence="false" lspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=|\theta|\Pr(y_{1}\neq y_{2})=2|\theta|\,\pi_{\theta}(a\mid x)\pi_{\theta}(b\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E15Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=2|\theta|\,\sigma(\theta)\sigma(-\theta)=\frac{2|\theta|e^{\theta}}{(1+e^{\theta})^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=2|\theta|\,\sigma(\theta)\sigma(-\theta)=\frac{2|\theta|e^{\theta}}{(1+e^{\theta})^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Convexity would imply <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(1)\leq\tfrac{1}{2}(R(0)+R(2))=\tfrac{1}{2}R(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(1)\leq\tfrac{1}{2}(R(0)+R(2))=\tfrac{1}{2}R(2)</annotation></semantics></math>, but using (<a href="#S4.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ Example 4.4. ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) we obtain</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(1)-\tfrac{1}{2}R(2)=\frac{2e(e-1)(e^{3}-1)}{(1+e)^{2}(1+e^{2})^{2}}&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(1)-\tfrac{1}{2}R(2)=\frac{2e(e-1)(e^{3}-1)}{(1+e)^{2}(1+e^{2})^{2}}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is not convex in general.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given the nonconvexity exhibited above, we control the curvature of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> through weak convexity: <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex if <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f(\cdot)+\tfrac{\kappa}{2}\|\cdot\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><msup><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\cdot)+\tfrac{\kappa}{2}\|\cdot\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex.
First we introduce several assumptions required for the analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 4</span></span><span id="Thmassumption4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Finite response set and bounded features)<span id="Thmassumption4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmassumption4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The response space <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, and the feature map satisfies <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\psi(x,y)\|\leq B_{\psi}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\psi(x,y)\|\leq B_{\psi}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="Thmassumption4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)\in X\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)\in X\times Y</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The boundedness in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (Finite response set and bounded features). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> is standard in analyses of log-linear and softmax models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2022)</a></cite>. Without loss of generality, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> can be normalized to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> by rescaling the feature map. However, we keep <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> explicit to highlight its impact on the weak convexity constant.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 5</span></span><span id="Thmassumption5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Bounded feasible set)<span id="Thmassumption5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The feasible set <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> is nonempty, closed, convex, and bounded. In particular, with <math id="Thmassumption5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Eq.(<a href="#S4.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), define</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D\ :=\ \sup_{\theta\in\Theta}\|\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}\|&lt;\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.500em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D\ :=\ \sup_{\theta\in\Theta}\|\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{ref}}\|&lt;\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5 (Bounded feasible set). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> also ensures that the Euclidean projection operator onto <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\Theta}(u):=\arg\min_{\theta\in\Theta}\|\theta-u\|_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\Theta}(u):=\arg\min_{\theta\in\Theta}\|\theta-u\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math>
is well-defined and nonexpansive<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bauschke and Combettes (2020)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.7</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Weak convexity of the robust penalty)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Log-linear policy class). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (Finite response set and bounded features). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5 (Bounded feasible set). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the robust penalty <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:\Theta\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:\Theta\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ Theorem 4.1 (Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃)). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, with</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{R}\leq 16B_{\psi}^{2}+4DB_{\psi}^{3}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{R}\leq 16B_{\psi}^{2}+4DB_{\psi}^{3}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 4.7 (Weak convexity of the robust penalty). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Write <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\theta}(z):=\mu(x)\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}|x)\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\theta}(z):=\mu(x)\pi_{\theta}(y_{1}|x)\pi_{\theta}(y_{2}|x)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\Pi_{\theta}}|s_{\theta}(z)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\Pi_{\theta}}|s_{\theta}(z)|</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\theta}(z):=(\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{ref}})^{\top}(\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\theta}(z):=(\theta-\theta_{\mathrm{ref}})^{\top}(\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>. First, we smooth the absolute value via <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\varepsilon}(u):=\sqrt{u^{2}+\varepsilon^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\varepsilon}(u):=\sqrt{u^{2}+\varepsilon^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and considers <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varepsilon}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\Pi_{\theta}}[\varphi_{\varepsilon}(s_{\theta}(z))]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varepsilon}(\theta):=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\Pi_{\theta}}[\varphi_{\varepsilon}(s_{\theta}(z))]</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is finite , <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite sum and derivatives can be exchanged with expectation. A log-derivative (score-function) calculation expresses <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}R_{\varepsilon}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}R_{\varepsilon}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as an expectation of terms involving: (i) derivatives of <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi_{\varepsilon}\circ s_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi_{\varepsilon}\circ s_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, (ii) the score <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{\theta}(z):=\nabla_{\theta}\log\Pi_{\theta}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{\theta}(z):=\nabla_{\theta}\log\Pi_{\theta}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> and its Jacobian. Bounded features bounds <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|S_{\theta}(z)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|S_{\theta}(z)\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla_{\theta}S_{\theta}(z)\|_{\mathrm{op}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>op</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla_{\theta}S_{\theta}(z)\|_{\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math>, while bounded parameter set bound <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2})\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\psi(x,y_{1})-\psi(x,y_{2})\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|s_{\theta}(z)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|s_{\theta}(z)|</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly on <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining these bounds yields a uniform lower bound <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{2}R_{\varepsilon}(\theta)\succeq-\kappa_{\varepsilon}I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⪰</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{2}R_{\varepsilon}(\theta)\succeq-\kappa_{\varepsilon}I</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\varepsilon}:=8GB_{\psi}+4MDB_{\psi}+2M\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\varepsilon}:=8GB_{\psi}+4MDB_{\psi}+2M\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>ε</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex, and letting <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\downarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\downarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> preserves weak convexity with <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{R}\leq 16B_{\psi}^{2}+4DB_{\psi}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>B</mi><mi>ψ</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{R}\leq 16B_{\psi}^{2}+4DB_{\psi}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.8</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Weak convexity of the composite objective )<span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Nondegenerate true oracle and admissible radius). ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption2" title="Assumption 2 (Log-linear policy class). ‣ 4.1 Decomposition of 𝐿^𝑊_𝜌⁢(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Smooth SAIL objective). ‣ 4.2 Regularity of of 𝐿^SAIL . ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption4" title="Assumption 4 (Finite response set and bounded features). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5 (Bounded feasible set). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the robust objective
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, with</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:=L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}+\lambda\kappa_{R},\qquad\lambda=\rho\beta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:=L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}+\lambda\kappa_{R},\qquad\lambda=\rho\beta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Smooth SAIL objective). ‣ 4.2 Regularity of of 𝐿^SAIL . ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz gradient and is therefore <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex. By Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 4.7 (Weak convexity of the robust penalty). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.7</span></a>, <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex on <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda R</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda\kappa_{R})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda\kappa_{R})</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex. The sum of weakly convex functions is weakly convex with parameter given by the sum of the parameters, yielding <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}+\lambda\kappa_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}+\lambda\kappa_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}+\lambda R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}=L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}+\lambda R</annotation></semantics></math> .
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Algorithm development</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We optimize the oracle-robust alignment objective <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> over the bounded convex parameter set <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>. To enforce feasibility explicitly, we consider the constrained objective</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)\ :=\ L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta),\qquad I_{\Theta}(\theta)\ :=\ \begin{cases}0,&amp;\theta\in\Theta,\\
+\infty,&amp;\theta\notin\Theta.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∉</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)\ :=\ L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta),\qquad I_{\Theta}(\theta)\ :=\ \begin{cases}0,&amp;\theta\in\Theta,\\
+\infty,&amp;\theta\notin\Theta.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.9</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorem 4.4, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is convex, the constrained objective <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) is also <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex with the same constant <math id="S4.Thmtheorem9.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> may be nonsmooth and only weakly convex, we measure first-order stationarity using the Moreau envelope<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moreau (1965)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.10</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Moreau envelope and proximal point)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fix <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>. The Moreau envelope of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\ :=\ \min_{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\{F(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|u-\theta\|^{2}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\ :=\ \min_{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\{F(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|u-\theta\|^{2}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the associated proximal point mapping is</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta)\ :=\ \arg\min_{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\{F(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|u-\theta\|^{2}\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta)\ :=\ \arg\min_{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\left\{F(u)+\frac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|u-\theta\|^{2}\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.3" class="ltx_p">When convenient, we write <math id="S4.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}:=\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}:=\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.11</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Properties of the Moreau envelope <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Davis and Drusvyatskiy, 2018</a>)</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex and bounded below, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then:</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is finite and continuously differentiable on </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Its gradient is </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)=\frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\bigl(\theta-\hat{\theta}\bigr)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)=\frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\bigl(\theta-\hat{\theta}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
and in particular,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\theta-\hat{\theta}\|\ =\ \lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\theta-\hat{\theta}\|\ =\ \lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The gradient </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is Lipschitz with constant</span></p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{env}}\ :=\ \frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{env}}\ :=\ \frac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Approximate stationarity) For </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}=\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}=\operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}\bigl(0,\partial F(\hat{\theta})\bigr)\ \leq\ \|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}\bigl(0,\partial F(\hat{\theta})\bigr)\ \leq\ \|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a> motivates <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|</annotation></semantics></math> as a smooth stationarity measure: controlling <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|</annotation></semantics></math> ensures (i) <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> is close to its proximal point <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> via (<a href="#S4.E19" title="Equation 19 ‣ Item 2 ‣ Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) and (ii) <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is nearly stationary for the original constrained problem via (<a href="#S4.E21" title="Equation 21 ‣ Item 4 ‣ Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>). The envelope parameter <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> governs the smoothing–bias trade-off: smaller <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> reduces smoothing bias but increases <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Item 3 ‣ Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 6</span></span><span id="Thmassumption6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Stochastic gradient/subgradient oracles)<span id="Thmassumption6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the policy-induced sampling distribution on <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=X\times Y\times Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=X\times Y\times Y</annotation></semantics></math> from Eq. (<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). At iteration <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> samples a mini-batch <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}=\{z_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}=\{z_{i}\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> with i.i.d. draws <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}\sim d_{\theta_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}\sim d_{\theta_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist measurable mappings</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}:\Theta\times Z^{B}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},\qquad G^{R}:\Theta\times Z^{B}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mi>B</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}:\Theta\times Z^{B}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},\qquad G^{R}:\Theta\times Z^{B}\to\mathbb{R}^{d},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">such that for all <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta;Z_{t})\mid\theta\right]=\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta),\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[G^{R}(\theta;Z_{t})\mid\theta\right]\in\partial R(\theta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta;Z_{t})\mid\theta\right]=\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta),\;\mathbb{E}\!\left[G^{R}(\theta;Z_{t})\mid\theta\right]\in\partial R(\theta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, there exist constants <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{SAIL}},\sigma^{2}_{R}&lt;\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>SAIL</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{SAIL}},\sigma^{2}_{R}&lt;\infty</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bigl\|G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta;Z_{t})-\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)\bigr\|^{2}\mid\theta\right]\leq\frac{\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{SAIL}}}{B}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>SAIL</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bigl\|G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta;Z_{t})-\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)\bigr\|^{2}\mid\theta\right]\leq\frac{\sigma^{2}_{\mathrm{SAIL}}}{B},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S4.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[\operatorname{dist}^{2}\!\Bigl(G^{R}(\theta;Z_{t}),\partial R(\theta)\Bigr)\mid\theta\right]\leq\frac{\sigma^{2}_{R}}{B}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>dist</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[\operatorname{dist}^{2}\!\Bigl(G^{R}(\theta;Z_{t}),\partial R(\theta)\Bigr)\mid\theta\right]\leq\frac{\sigma^{2}_{R}}{B}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmassumption6.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption6.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Finally define the composite direction <math id="Thmassumption6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\theta;Z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\theta;Z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S4.E22" title="Equation 22 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 4.12</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6 (Stochastic gradient/subgradient oracles). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> instantiates the standard <em id="S4.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">stochastic first-order oracle</em> model.
Such assumptions are ubiquitous in the analysis of stochastic approximation and stochastic (sub)gradient methods; see, e.g.,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghadimi and Lan (2013)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agarwal et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2024)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use a projected stochastic composite gradient method to minimize <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>). At iterate <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample prompts and response pairs according to the policy-induced sampling in Eq.(<a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>): draw <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1},y_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta_{t}}(\cdot\mid x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1},y_{2}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta_{t}}(\cdot\mid x)</annotation></semantics></math>, forming a triple <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(x,y_{1},y_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. For the SAIL term <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, we additionally query a preference label from the oracle, which determines the ordered pair <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{w},y_{\ell})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{w},y_{\ell})</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, a Bernoulli label <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> indicating whether <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{1}\succ y_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≻</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1}\succ y_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). Using a mini-batch <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> i.i.d. triples, we form a stochastic gradient estimator for <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and a stochastic (sub)gradient estimator for the robust penalty <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> , and combine them with weight <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\rho\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\rho\beta</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\ :=\ G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})+\lambda\,G^{R}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\ :=\ G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})+\lambda\,G^{R}(\theta_{t};Z_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The constrained formulation then yields the projected update</p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}\ :=\ \mathrm{proj}_{\Theta}\!\left(\theta_{t}-\eta\,G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>proj</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}\ :=\ \mathrm{proj}_{\Theta}\!\left(\theta_{t}-\eta\,G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{proj}_{\Theta}(v):=\arg\min_{u\in\Theta}\|u-v\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>proj</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{proj}_{\Theta}(v):=\arg\min_{u\in\Theta}\|u-v\|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a stepsize. The projection ensures <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, so the composite objective <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=L^{W}_{\rho}+I_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=L^{W}_{\rho}+I_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined along the iterates.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Robust Stochastic Composite Gradient Descent (R-SCGD)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}\in\Theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}\in\Theta</annotation></semantics></math>, stepsize <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, horizon <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, weight <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\rho\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\rho\beta</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,1,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,1,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
              Sample a mini-batch <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{t}=\{(x_{i},y_{1,i},y_{2,i})\}_{i=1}^{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{t}=\{(x_{i},y_{1,i},y_{2,i})\}_{i=1}^{B}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\sim\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\sim\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="y_{1,i},y_{2,i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta_{t}}(\cdot\mid x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mover><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{1,i},y_{2,i}\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\mathrm{i.i.d.}}}{{\sim}}\pi_{\theta_{t}}(\cdot\mid x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
              Query preference labels for the sampled pairs to construct <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y_{w,i},y_{\ell,i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y_{w,i},y_{\ell,i})</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              Compute stochastic oracles <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{R}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{R}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, and form <math id="alg1.l5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})=G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})+\lambda G^{R}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>R</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})=G^{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})+\lambda G^{R}(\theta_{t};Z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              Update <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathrm{proj}_{\Theta}\!\bigl(\theta_{t}-\eta\,G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>proj</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}\leftarrow\mathrm{proj}_{\Theta}\!\bigl(\theta_{t}-\eta\,G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
             Sample <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\sim\mathrm{Unif}\{0,1,\dots,T-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\sim\mathrm{Unif}\{0,1,\dots,T-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
             <span id="alg1.l9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> .

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Analysis</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Convergence analysis</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for the constrained objective <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E16" title="Equation 16 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) using the Moreau-envelope stationarity measure from Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="Definition 4.10 (Moreau envelope and proximal point). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>. Throughout, we use the weak convexity constant <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Weak convexity of the composite objective ). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a> and assume the smoothness condition on <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\mathrm{SAIL}}</annotation></semantics></math> stated in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption3" title="Assumption 3 (Smooth SAIL objective). ‣ 4.2 Regularity of of 𝐿^SAIL . ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmassumption7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmassumption7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 7</span></span><span id="Thmassumption7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lower bounded objective)<span id="Thmassumption7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmassumption7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmassumption7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmassumption7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The constrained objective <math id="Thmassumption7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)=L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)=L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is proper, lower semicontinuous, and bounded from below on <math id="Thmassumption7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>; denote</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\inf}\ :=\ \inf_{\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}F(\theta)\ &gt;\ -\infty." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.500em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">&gt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\inf}\ :=\ \inf_{\theta\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}F(\theta)\ &gt;\ -\infty.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Lower boundedness is needed to telescope descent inequalities for the Moreau envelope and is natural for constrained likelihood-based objectives on a bounded parameter set.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Main Result:</span>
With the above assumptions in place, we are ready to present the main theoretical results of this work.
We first establish the convergence guarantee for Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, and then derive the corresponding oracle (sample) complexity bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convergence rate for the Moreau envelope)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let Assumptions <a href="#Thmassumption1" title="Assumption 1 (Nondegenerate true oracle and admissible radius). ‣ 3 Problem Setup ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>–<a href="#Thmassumption7" title="Assumption 7 (Lower bounded objective). ‣ 5.1 Convergence analysis ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> hold and let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> be the weak convexity constant from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Weak convexity of the composite objective ). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Fix any <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> and run Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations with stepsize <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\sim\mathrm{Unif}\{0,1,\dots,T-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\sim\mathrm{Unif}\{0,1,\dots,T-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the output index and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding iterate. Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma B.4 (Second-moment bound for gradient oracle). ‣ B.4 Auxiliary lemma for stochastic gradient oracle ‣ Appendix B Weak convexity: Proofs of Theorems 4.7 and 4.8 ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> implies that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|G(\theta;Z)\|^{2}\mid\theta\right]\leq G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\qquad\forall\theta\in\Theta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mspace style="width:2.167em;" width="2.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|G(\theta;Z)\|^{2}\mid\theta\right]\leq G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\qquad\forall\theta\in\Theta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a generic mini-batch of size <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> drawn i.i.d. from <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the explicit
constant given in Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma B.4 (Second-moment bound for gradient oracle). ‣ B.4 Auxiliary lemma for stochastic gradient oracle ‣ Appendix B Weak convexity: Proofs of Theorems 4.7 and 4.8 ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.. Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bigl\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\bigr\|^{2}\right]\ \leq\ \frac{F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}}{\eta(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\,T}\;+\;\frac{L_{\mathrm{env}}\,\eta}{2(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})}\,G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.502em" rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bigl\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\bigr\|^{2}\right]\ \leq\ \frac{F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}}{\eta(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\,T}\;+\;\frac{L_{\mathrm{env}}\,\eta}{2(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})}\,G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Item 3 ‣ Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Sample complexity for envelope stationarity)<span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under the conditions of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Convergence rate for the Moreau envelope). ‣ 5.1 Convergence analysis ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>, fix <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> and set</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\ :=\ \sqrt{\frac{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\,T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\ :=\ \sqrt{\frac{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\,T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> guarantees</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bigl\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\bigr\|^{2}\right]\ \leq\ \sqrt{\frac{2\,G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})^{3}\,T}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.500em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\left[\bigl\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\bigr\|^{2}\right]\ \leq\ \sqrt{\frac{2\,G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})^{3}\,T}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, to achieve <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|^{2}]\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|^{2}]\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, it suffices to take</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex26X"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\ \frac{2\,G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})^{3}\,\varepsilon^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\ \frac{2\,G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})^{3}\,\varepsilon^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex26Xa"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex26Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{8\left(C+\frac{\sigma_{\mathrm{SAIL}}^{2}+\lambda^{2}\sigma_{R}^{2}}{B}\right)\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})^{3}\,\varepsilon^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>SAIL</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{8\left(C+\frac{\sigma_{\mathrm{SAIL}}^{2}+\lambda^{2}\sigma_{R}^{2}}{B}\right)\bigl(F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}\bigr)}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})^{3}\,\varepsilon^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C=G_{\nabla\mathrm{SAIL}}^{2}+\lambda^{2}G_{\partial R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>SAIL</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C=G_{\nabla\mathrm{SAIL}}^{2}+\lambda^{2}G_{\partial R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are constant as defined in Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma B.4 (Second-moment bound for gradient oracle). ‣ B.4 Auxiliary lemma for stochastic gradient oracle ‣ Appendix B Weak convexity: Proofs of Theorems 4.7 and 4.8 ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. If we set <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=\widetilde{O}(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=\widetilde{O}(1)</annotation></semantics></math>,then we obtain a sample complexity of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BT=\widetilde{O}(\varepsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BT=\widetilde{O}(\varepsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch of Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Convergence rate for the Moreau envelope). ‣ 5.1 Convergence analysis ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Fix <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> and write <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{t}:=\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{t}:=\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math>-smooth with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{env}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{env}}</annotation></semantics></math> given in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="Equation 20 ‣ Item 3 ‣ Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>). One establishes a one-step descent inequality for the projected update (<a href="#S4.E23" title="Equation 23 ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>) by combining:
(i) smoothness of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math> to upper bound <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\xi_{t},G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\xi_{t},G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>;
(ii) the fact that projection cannot increase the envelope value for <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=f+I_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=f+I_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math>;
and (iii) a weak-convexity monotonicity inequality relating <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\xi_{t},v_{t}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\xi_{t},v_{t}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\xi_{t}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\xi_{t}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{t}:=\mathbb{E}[G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\mid\theta_{t}]\in\partial F(\theta_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">∈</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{t}:=\mathbb{E}[G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\mid\theta_{t}]\in\partial F(\theta_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption6" title="Assumption 6 (Stochastic gradient/subgradient oracles). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Taking conditional expectations and using <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\|^{2}\mid\theta_{t}]\leq G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\|^{2}\mid\theta_{t}]\leq G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> yields</span></p>
<table id="S5.E25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S5.E25X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t+1})\mid\theta_{t}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t+1})\mid\theta_{t}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E25X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t})-\eta(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\|\xi_{t}\|^{2}+\frac{L_{\mathrm{env}}\eta^{2}}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\|^{2}\mid\theta_{t}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{t})-\eta(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\|\xi_{t}\|^{2}+\frac{L_{\mathrm{env}}\eta^{2}}{2}\,\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|G(\theta_{t};Z_{t})\|^{2}\mid\theta_{t}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Summing over <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0,\dots,T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0,\dots,T-1</annotation></semantics></math> gives a telescoping bound on <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}[\|\xi_{t}\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\mathbb{E}[\|\xi_{t}\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>inf</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{0})-F_{\inf}</annotation></semantics></math> (Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption7" title="Assumption 7 (Lower bounded objective). ‣ 5.1 Convergence analysis ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\eta^{2}G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>G</mi><mi>tot</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\eta^{2}G_{\mathrm{tot}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, selecting <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly from <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0,\dots,T-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0,\dots,T-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> converts the average to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding (<a href="#S5.E24" title="Equation 24 ‣ Theorem 5.2 (Convergence rate for the Moreau envelope). ‣ 5.1 Convergence analysis ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>On the practical role of the proximal point</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis measures stationarity of the <em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">constrained</em> robust objective</p>
<table id="S6.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)\ :=\ L_{\rho}^{W}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)\ :=\ L_{\rho}^{W}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">via the Moreau envelope <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\in(0,1/\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math> (Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem10" title="Definition 4.10 (Moreau envelope and proximal point). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.10</span></a>), where <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is the weak convexity constant from Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 4.8 (Weak convexity of the composite objective ). ‣ 4.3 Weak convexity of 𝑅(𝜃) ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.8</span></a>. Algorithm 1 outputs a uniformly random iterate <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>. The theory (Theorem <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 5.2 (Convergence rate for the Moreau envelope). ‣ 5.1 Convergence analysis ‣ 5 Analysis ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>) certifies <em id="S6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">envelope stationarity</em> at <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> by controlling <math id="S6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, rather than directly bounding <math id="S6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\theta_{R}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\theta_{R}))</annotation></semantics></math>. The interpretation is instead through the associated proximal point</p>
<table id="S6.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{R}\ :=\ \operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta_{R})\ \in\ \arg\min_{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\Big\{F(u)+\tfrac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|u-\theta_{R}\|_{2}^{2}\Big\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>prox</mi><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{R}\ :=\ \operatorname{prox}_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}F}(\theta_{R})\ \in\ \arg\min_{u\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\Big\{F(u)+\tfrac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|u-\theta_{R}\|_{2}^{2}\Big\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which is unique since <math id="S6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weakly convex and <math id="S6.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}&lt;1/\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}&lt;1/\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 4.11 (Properties of the Moreau envelope ( , )). ‣ 4.4 Algorithm development ‣ 4 Proposed Approach ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.11</span></a> provides the key link between envelope stationarity and proximity:</p>
<table id="S6.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\theta_{R}-\hat{\theta}_{R}\|_{2}\ =\ \lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\theta_{R}-\hat{\theta}_{R}\|_{2}\ =\ \lambda_{\mathrm{env}}\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">and moreover <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\hat{\theta}_{R}))\leq\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\hat{\theta}_{R}))\leq\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, small <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneously implies (i) <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> lies in a small neighborhood of <math id="S6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) <math id="S6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is nearly first-order stationary for the original constrained objective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A practical issue is that computing <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly still requires solving the (generally nontrivial) strongly convex proximal subproblem above. Even when <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is small (hence <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is nearby), proximal operators frequently do not admit closed forms and are evaluated via iterative inner solvers, which can be expensive in realistic models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salzo and Villa (2012)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma D.1 (What if we do not compute the proximal point?). ‣ Appendix D On the practical role of the proximal point ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> shows that while <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\theta))\geq(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\theta))\geq(1-\kappa\lambda_{\mathrm{env}})\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|</annotation></semantics></math> always holds, under <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-weak convexity alone there is <em id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no</em> universal constant <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\theta))\leq C\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\theta))\leq C\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta)\|</annotation></semantics></math>
for all <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> (even when <math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0</annotation></semantics></math>). This motivates phrasing guarantees in terms of the proximal point <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> rather than the raw iterate <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, one can compute an <em id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inexact</em> proximal refinement <math id="S6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\theta}_{R}\approx\hat{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\theta}_{R}\approx\hat{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> via a warm-started inner loop on
<math id="S6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Psi_{R}(\theta):=F(\theta)+\tfrac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|\theta-\theta_{R}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi_{R}(\theta):=F(\theta)+\tfrac{1}{2\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|\theta-\theta_{R}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,where proximal subproblems are solved only up
to a prescribed accuracy (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt et al. 2011</a></cite>).Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Practical computation of the proximal point ‣ Appendix D On the practical role of the proximal point ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> formalizes this viewpoint for our setting and provides a residual-based stopping rule.
Lemma <a href="#A4.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma D.3 (Stationarity of an inexact proximal point). ‣ A simple inner-loop solver. ‣ D.1 Practical computation of the proximal point ‣ Appendix D On the practical role of the proximal point ‣ Oracle-Robust Online Alignment for Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a> yields the practical takeaway: if <math id="S6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\theta}_{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\theta}_{R}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the proximal residual condition
<math id="S6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial\Psi_{R}(\bar{\theta}_{R}))\leq\varepsilon_{\mathrm{prox}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>prox</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial\Psi_{R}(\bar{\theta}_{R}))\leq\varepsilon_{\mathrm{prox}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then</p>
<table id="S6.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\bar{\theta}_{R}))\ \leq\ \|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}\ +\ \varepsilon_{\mathrm{prox}}\ +\ \tfrac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|\bar{\theta}_{R}-\hat{\theta}_{R}\|_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>dist</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.722em">+</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>prox</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.722em">+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{dist}(0,\partial F(\bar{\theta}_{R}))\ \leq\ \|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}\ +\ \varepsilon_{\mathrm{prox}}\ +\ \tfrac{1}{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}\|\bar{\theta}_{R}-\hat{\theta}_{R}\|_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">Consequently, controlling <math id="S6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}(\theta_{R})\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> together with the inexactness terms <math id="S6.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\mathrm{prox}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>prox</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\mathrm{prox}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\bar{\theta}_{R}-\hat{\theta}_{R}\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>θ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>R</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\bar{\theta}_{R}-\hat{\theta}_{R}\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> yields an explicit near-stationarity certificate for the original objective <math id="S6.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We studied <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">oracle-robust</em> online alignment of language models, where preference feedback is collected on-policy but the true preference oracle can deviate from the assumed model in a structured, worst-case manner. We introduced a pointwise oracle uncertainty set <math id="S7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> and defined the robust objective <math id="S7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> as the worst-case negative log-likelihood over <math id="S7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mi>W</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in U^{W}(P^{\star},\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>. Our main novelty is an <em id="S7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exact closed-form decomposition</em> of this otherwise difficult minimax objective: <math id="S7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)=L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)+\lambda R(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=\rho\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=\rho\beta</annotation></semantics></math>; We cast the constrained problem as minimizing <math id="S7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\theta)=L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>L</mi><mi>ρ</mi><mi>W</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\theta)=L^{W}_{\rho}(\theta)+I_{\Theta}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>, analyze it as a weakly convex composite objective, and measure stationarity using the Moreau envelope <math id="S7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We establish a <math id="S7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{O}(\varepsilon^{-2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>O</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{O}(\varepsilon^{-2})</annotation></semantics></math> stochastic-oracle (sample) complexity for reaching an <math id="S7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stationary point of <math id="S7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>env</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\lambda_{\mathrm{env}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in turn implies proximity to a nearly stationary point of the original constrained robust objective; as a special case, setting <math id="S7.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0</annotation></semantics></math> recovers a convergence-to-stationarity guarantee for optimizing <math id="S7.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>SAIL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathrm{SAIL}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
